        {
          "id": "notes_scope",
          "title": "Join keys, reconciliation, and grain (notes below the diagram)",
          "width": "full",
          "type": "markdown",
          "markdown": "**June reconciliation (USD)**\n\n- Gross sales $64,130.00 − discounts $4,704.40 − refunds $5,408.80 = net revenue $54,016.80\n- Net revenue $54,016.80 − product cost $24,183.00 − fulfillment cost $3,244.50 − return processing cost $433.50 = profit before marketing $26,155.80\n- Profit before marketing $26,155.80 − marketing spend $17,065.25 = contribution profit $9,090.55; contribution margin = 9,090.55 / 54,016.80 = 0.1683 (16.83%)\n\n**Key join keys**\n\n- Product rollup: `order_items JOIN products ON p.id = oi.product_id`, then `GROUP BY oi.order_id`.\n- Return rollup: `returns JOIN order_items ON oi.id = r.order_item_id`, then `GROUP BY oi.order_id` (a separate subquery from the product rollup).\n- Order-level view: `orders o JOIN it ON it.order_id = o.id` (inner join), `LEFT JOIN rt ON rt.order_id = o.id` (`COALESCE` fills 0 when there are no returns).\n- Channel rollup and spend: `oe.order_month = ms.month AND oe.channel = ms.channel`; `marketing_spend` is unique on (month, channel), so spend is deducted only once at month×channel grain and is never multiplied by order rows.\n\n**Grain and definition distinctions**\n\n- Row counts are not interchangeable: June product lines = 666 rows (order_items, mapping to 375 orders); order level = 375 rows (order_economics, 1 row/order); month×channel rollup = 5 rows = 5 channels (marketing_spend is also 5 rows in June); June return records = 51 rows.\n- Refunds and return processing cost are different: refunds of $5,408.80 reduce net revenue, while processing cost of $433.50 is deducted as a separate cost.\n- All amounts are USD; fixed salaries and administrative expenses are outside the contribution profit definition; `customers` and `promotion_code` do not participate in the calculation."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
